### 알터 스토어 (System Store)
{{/if}}

Access & Interface: A personal interface separate from the Status Window, visible only to its owner. Available only from 4th-generation Hunters onward. Not accessed via any device or terminal.
Currency: 'Coin', gained through Hunter activities — no real money.
Available Items: Equipment (weapons, armor) and consumables (potions, scrolls). Personalized recommendations tailored to each Hunter.
Stock & Availability: Inventory changes unpredictably; items may vanish anytime.
Item Delivery: Purchased items appear instantly near the Hunter.
Administrator / Operator: Unknown. The Hunter Association is not the operator and does not understand its mechanism or origin. All attempts to trace it have failed.
{{/if}}

### 'Hunter'

{{#if_pure {{equal::{{getvar::lang}}::0}}}}
- Description: Awakened humans with a 'Status Window', 'System Message'{{#if_pure {{equal::{{getvar::lore}}::1}}}}, and 'Alter Store'{{/if}} (self-only, non-shareable). These appear as floating midair windows of unknown origin. Hunters wield supernatural powers to fight Gate monsters and explore dungeons, forming a distinct class.
{{/if}}
{{#if_pure {{equal::{{getvar::lang}}::1}}}}
- Description: Awakened humans with a '상태창', '시스템 메시지'{{#if_pure {{equal::{{getvar::lore}}::1}}}}, and '알터 스토어'{{/if}} (self-only, non-shareable). These appear as floating midair windows of unknown origin. Hunters wield supernatural powers to fight Gate monsters and explore dungeons, forming a distinct class.
{{/if}}

#### History

- 1st Generation: The first Awakened humans appear + Status Window visible to a rare few (~120 years ago).
- 2nd Generation: Combat roles (positions) are set + the first Hidden Class user appears. (~80 years ago)
- 3rd Generation: The nation's first 'Large Guilds' form + the Hunter ecosystem stabilizes (~30 years ago. Few are still active today).
- 4th Generation: {{#if_pure {{equal::{{getvar::lore}}::1}}}}The 'Alter Store/Coin' emerges + {{/if}}a systematic Hunter system is established.

#### Ranking System

- Ranks: E (lowest) to S (highest). No 'F' Rank.

#### Activity & Rank Maintenance
- Active status requires regular Association assessments.
- Re-assessment may be requested once per week.
- Mandatory re-assessments must be accepted; refusal revokes the license.

#### Gate Access & Information

- Hunters use Association terminals to check active Gates (location, danger level).
- Gate entry requires explicit terminal approval; unauthorized access is forbidden.

### Hunter Ranks (Average)

- E-Rank: Weakest, struggle even with E-Gates solo. Basic skills.{{#if_pure {{equal::{{getvar::stats}}::1}}}} Avg. main stats 10-25, Other ~10-15.{{/if}}
- D-Rank: Can handle E-Gates, okay in D-Gate parties; can fight D-tier monsters 1v1.{{#if_pure {{equal::{{getvar::stats}}::1}}}} Avg. main stats 25-40, Other ~15-20.{{/if}}
- C-Rank: Core for C-Gates, can handle multiple D-monsters; "veteran" territory.{{#if_pure {{equal::{{getvar::stats}}::1}}}} Avg. main stats 40-60, Other ~20-30.{{/if}}
- B-Rank: Key for B-Gates, dominate C-monsters; near superhuman.{{#if_pure {{equal::{{getvar::stats}}::1}}}} Avg. main stats 60-80, Other ~30-40.{{/if}}
- A-Rank: Can solo B-Gates, lead A-Gate raids; nationally important.{{#if_pure {{equal::{{getvar::stats}}::1}}}} Avg. main stats 80-100, Other ~40-50.{{/if}}
- S-Rank: Essential for S-Gates, can handle multiple A-monsters; global strategic assets.{{#if_pure {{equal::{{getvar::stats}}::1}}}} Avg. main stats 100+.{{/if}}

### Key Points:

{{#if_pure {{equal::{{getvar::stats}}::1}}}}- Rank vs. Level: Rank (measuring Mana/potential) is separate from Level. High Level ≠ high Rank; leveling boosts potential for rank growth.{{/if}}
- Variables: Power varies by{{#if_pure {{equal::{{getvar::stats}}::1}}}} Stats (Tank vs. Dealer),{{/if}} Class (Hidden classes often have higher potential and are common among S-Ranks), Skills (Unique skills are game-changers), Gear (Rare/Unique gear boosts power), and Experience/Strategy.
- Define a power scale from Rank S to Rank E. A two-rank gap is absolute—the higher rank is on another level (e.g., a C-rank Hunter crushes an E-rank monster). Mutant monsters count as one rank higher by default, and rare mutants can exceed their tier and climb further based on strength.

